
# React Hooks — Complete Beginner Examples

## 1. `useState` — Store and update data

```
import { useState } from "react";

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Count: {count}</h2>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Increase
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Use: Counter, form inputs, login status, and other changing values.

## 2. `useEffect` — Run code after rendering

```
import { useEffect } from "react";

function App() {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.log("Component loaded");
  }, []);

  return <h2>Hello React</h2>;
}

export default App;
```

Use: API calls, timers, and synchronization with external systems.

- — run when the component mounts.
- `[count]` — run when `count` changes.
- No dependency array — run after every render.

In development Strict Mode, React may run an extra setup and cleanup cycle.

## 3. `useContext` — Share data between components

```
import { createContext, useContext } from "react";

const NameContext = createContext();

function Child() {
  const name = useContext(NameContext);

  return <h2>Hello {name}</h2>;
}

function App() {
  return (
    <NameContext.Provider value="Ravi">
      <Child />
    </NameContext.Provider>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Output: `Hello Ravi`

Use: Share user details, themes, or language settings without passing props through every intermediate component.

## 4. `useRef` — Store a value or access an element

```
import { useRef } from "react";

function App() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  function focusInput() {
    inputRef.current.focus();
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <input ref={inputRef} placeholder="Enter name" />
      <button onClick={focusInput}>Focus Input</button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Use: Focus input fields, access DOM elements, or store values that don't need to trigger a render.

## 5. `useReducer` — Manage state using actions

```
import { useReducer } from "react";

function reducer(count, action) {
  if (action === "increase") return count + 1;
  if (action === "decrease") return count - 1;
  return count;
}

function App() {
  const [count, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, 0);

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{count}</h2>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch("increase")}>
        Increase
      </button>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch("decrease")}>
        Decrease
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Use: Manage more complex state and related actions.

## 6. `useMemo` — Cache a calculated value

```
import { useMemo, useState } from "react";

function App() {
  const [number, setNumber] = useState(2);

  const square = useMemo(() => {
    console.log("Calculating square");
    return number * number;
  }, [number]);

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Square: {square}</h2>
      <button onClick={() => setNumber(number + 1)}>
        Change Number
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Use: Avoid repeating expensive calculations when their dependencies haven't changed. For simple calculations, you usually don't need it.

## 7. `useCallback` — Cache a function

```
import { useCallback, useState } from "react";

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const sayHello = useCallback(() => {
    console.log("Hello React");
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{count}</h2>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
        Increase
      </button>
      <button onClick={sayHello}>
        Say Hello
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Use: Preserve a function reference between renders when useful, especially with memoized child components or other hook dependencies.

## 8. `useLayoutEffect` — Run an effect before browser repaint

```
import { useLayoutEffect, useRef } from "react";

function App() {
  const boxRef = useRef(null);

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    console.log(
      boxRef.current.getBoundingClientRect().width
    );
  }, []);

  return (
    <div ref={boxRef} style={{ width: 200 }}>
      Hello React
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Use: Measure or adjust layout before the browser paints. Prefer `useEffect` for most other side effects.

## 9. `useId` — Generate an ID for accessibility

```
import { useId } from "react";

function App() {
  const id = useId();

  return (
    <div>
      <label htmlFor={id}>Student Name</label>
      <input id={id} />
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Use: Connect labels to inputs and generate unique IDs for accessibility. Don't use it for list keys.

## 10. `useTransition` — Mark an update as non-urgent

```
import { useState, useTransition } from "react";

function App() {
  const [tab, setTab] = useState("Home");
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

  function changeTab() {
    startTransition(() => {
      setTab("Courses");
    });
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{tab}</h2>
      <button onClick={changeTab}>
        Show Courses
      </button>
      {isPending && <p>Loading update...</p>}
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Use: Keep urgent interactions responsive while React processes non-urgent UI updates. For this tiny example, it isn't necessary, and `isPending` may never visibly become true.

## 11. `useDeferredValue` — Defer updating a value

```
import { useState, useDeferredValue } from "react";

function App() {
  const [text, setText] = useState("");
  const deferredText = useDeferredValue(text);

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        value={text}
        onChange={(e) => setText(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Search courses"
      />

      <p>Deferred text: {deferredText}</p>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Use: Keep typing responsive when a dependent UI is expensive to render. It doesn't debounce API calls or guarantee a fixed delay.

## 12. `useImperativeHandle` — Customize a component's ref

This is an advanced hook, commonly used when a parent needs access to a specific method exposed by a child component.

```
import {
  useRef,
  useImperativeHandle,
  forwardRef
} from "react";

const MyInput = forwardRef(function MyInput(props, ref) {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({
    focus() {
      inputRef.current.focus();
    }
  }), []);

  return <input ref={inputRef} />;
});

function App() {
  const myRef = useRef(null);

  return (
    <div>
      <MyInput ref={myRef} />
      <button onClick={() => myRef.current.focus()}>
        Focus
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Use: Expose selected imperative methods to a parent instead of exposing the entire DOM element.

## 13. `useSyncExternalStore` — Subscribe to an external store

```
import { useSyncExternalStore } from "react";

let count = 0;
const listeners = new Set();

function subscribe(callback) {
  listeners.add(callback);
  return () => listeners.delete(callback);
}

function getSnapshot() {
  return count;
}

function increase() {
  count++;
  listeners.forEach((callback) => callback());
}

function App() {
  const value = useSyncExternalStore(
    subscribe,
    getSnapshot
  );

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Count: {value}</h2>
      <button onClick={increase}>Increase</button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Use: Subscribe to external stores or browser APIs that provide a subscription mechanism.

## 14. `useDebugValue` — Display information in React DevTools

```
import { useDebugValue, useState } from "react";

function useOnlineStatus() {
  const [isOnline] = useState(true);

  useDebugValue(isOnline ? "Online" : "Offline");

  return isOnline;
}

function App() {
  const isOnline = useOnlineStatus();

  return <h2>{isOnline ? "Online" : "Offline"}</h2>;
}

export default App;
```

Use: Help developers inspect custom hooks in React DevTools. This example uses a fixed initial status, not real network detection.

# 15. Custom Hook — Create your own reusable hook

A custom hook is a JavaScript function whose name starts with `use` and that can use other React hooks.

For example, let's create a reusable counter hook.

File 1: `useCounter.js`

```
import { useState } from "react";

function useCounter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  function increase() {
    setCount((previous) => previous + 1);
  }

  return { count, increase };
}

export default useCounter;
```

File 2: `App.jsx`

```
import useCounter from "./useCounter";

function App() {
  const { count, increase } = useCounter();

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Count: {count}</h2>
      <button onClick={increase}>
        Increase
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

Why custom hooks?

- Reuse logic across components.
- Keep components cleaner.
- Share stateful behavior without duplicating code.

Each component calling `useCounter()` gets its own independent counter state.

# 16. Bonus: `useInsertionEffect`

This is a specialized hook mainly intended for CSS-in-JS library authors to insert styles before layout effects run.

```
import { useInsertionEffect } from "react";

function App() {
  useInsertionEffect(() => {
    // Specialized style-insertion logic goes here.
  }, []);

  return <h2>Hello React</h2>;
}

export default App;
```

For normal application development, you generally won't need this hook.

## Quick revision table

| Hook                   | Simple purpose                          |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| `useState`             | Store changing data                     |
| `useEffect`            | Synchronize with external systems       |
| `useContext`           | Share data across components            |
| `useRef`               | Keep a value or access a DOM element    |
| `useReducer`           | Manage state with actions               |
| `useMemo`              | Cache a calculation                     |
| `useCallback`          | Cache a function                        |
| `useLayoutEffect`      | Work with layout before repaint         |
| `useId`                | Generate accessibility IDs              |
| `useTransition`        | Mark updates as non-urgent              |
| `useDeferredValue`     | Defer rendering a value                 |
| `useImperativeHandle`  | Expose selected methods through refs    |
| `useSyncExternalStore` | Subscribe to an external store          |
| `useDebugValue`        | Debug custom hooks                      |
| `useInsertionEffect`   | Insert styles for specialized libraries |
| Custom hooks           | Reuse React logic                       |

Important: React also provides newer APIs and specialized hooks in some versions. The examples above cover the main built-in hooks and the custom-hook pattern.

For beginners, focus on this order: `useState` → `useEffect` → `useContext` → `useRef` → custom hooks. Learn the remaining hooks after you are comfortable building basic React applications.